const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How much does shipping cost from China to Bahrain?',
    a: 'Zero verified Bahrain freight benchmarks are published — no dollar, per-CBM or per-kg figure — so every FCL, LCL, air, express and DDP line is a request-for-quote item. Ask a forwarder to itemise freight, surcharges, THC, documentation, inspection, clearance, 5% duty, 10% VAT and delivery, because rates vary by carrier, season, cargo and surcharge.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does it take to ship from China to Bahrain?',
    a: 'WorldFreightHub route data records a typical 22 days with a 15–30 day planning range from Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan or Shenzhen to Khalifa bin Salman (LOW confidence, because the route data is an estimate). No verified Bahrain air transit is published in the snapshot, so request that from your carrier rather than assuming the sea figure applies.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does Bahrain have no published rate benchmark in the snapshot?',
    a: 'The verified snapshot publishes zero Bahrain FCL, LCL, air, express or DDP rate benchmarks, because Bahrain is a smaller corridor than the UAE or Saudi Arabia. Borrowing a Jebel Ali, Dammam or Hamad bracket would be misleading, so every rate line is shown as a request-for-quote marker instead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is the Bahrain landed cost calculated?',
    a: 'For most goods, the baseline statutory landed cost is CIF × 1.05 × 1.10 = CIF × 1.155 — 5% customs duty on CIF first, then 10% VAT on the duty-inclusive base. Build the CIF value from cost, insurance and freight, then add destination THC, documentation, clearance, inland trucking and any demurrage/detention on top of that statutory figure.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Bahrain charge VAT on top of import duty?',
    a: 'Yes. Bahrain applies 10% VAT on the duty-inclusive base, so the statutory stack is CIF × 1.05 × 1.10 = CIF × 1.155. That is higher than the UAE/Oman 5% VAT compound (CIF × 1.1025) and below Saudi Arabia’s 15% VAT case. Verify the current rate and any zero-rating or exemption with the Bahrain tax authority.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you show a worked example for a CIF 10,000 USD Bahrain shipment?',
    a: 'Yes: customs duty is 10,000 × 5% = 500, giving a duty-inclusive base of 10,500. VAT is 10,500 × 10% = 1,050. The total before operational fees is 11,550. Operational fees such as brokerage, Bahrain processing, inspection, delivery and storage sit on top and should be requested as an itemised schedule.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the 5% duty apply to freight and insurance?',
    a: 'Yes — the 5% duty base is CIF, so each additional $1 of freight adds $0.05 duty and, after 10% VAT on the duty-inclusive base, another $0.105, or $0.155 all-in per extra dollar of freight. Freight and insurance are included in the value used for the duty calculation, and a higher freight rate therefore also raises the VAT line.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What hidden fees should I expect on a Bahrain shipment?',
    a: 'Expect nine cost categories beyond the freight rate — origin charges, destination THC and port charges, documentation and bill of lading fees, customs brokerage, Bahrain electronic processing, inspection, inland transport, cargo insurance and demurrage/detention after free time — before the statutory 5% CIF duty and 10% VAT. Specific fee amounts were not published in the research snapshot, so request an itemised schedule.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the cheapest way to ship from China to Bahrain?',
    a: 'For cargo that can absorb a 15–30 day window, sea freight is the unit-cost floor — but only after the ×1.155 duty+VAT stack and all destination fees are included in the all-in comparison. Air is rarely the cheapest option and is justified by speed, value density or urgency, not by cost.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is FCL or LCL better for shipping to Bahrain?',
    a: 'FCL and LCL split on the container-utilisation breakpoint: LCL charges per CBM and adds consolidation minimums and destination handling, while FCL wins as volume approaches a full-container share — but the snapshot publishes zero Bahrain FCL/LCL numbers. Request both all-in quotes and compare the full landed cost.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which port should be used in the landed-cost quote?',
    a: 'Use Khalifa bin Salman for containerised sea freight. Its UN/LOCODE is BHKBS. Mina Salman and Hidd are not in the WorldFreightHub Bahrain data, so a quote that uses one of them may be based on unverified routing. For air, request a Bahrain air-gateway quote.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does the HS code affect the Bahrain landed cost?',
    a: 'The HS code decides the duty line — the 5% GCC baseline, a possible Bahrain-specific exemption or higher line, or a prohibited/restricted classification — and can also affect VAT treatment. Bahrain-specific tariff lines are not published in the verified snapshot, so classify before quoting and confirm the line with Bahrain Customs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need SABER or SASO to import into Bahrain?',
    a: 'No. SABER and SASO are Saudi-only conformity systems. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a Bahrain quote. Bahrain may have its own standards or product-approval requirements for certain goods, but those are not published in the verified snapshot — confirm the applicable Bahrain process for your product.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does King Fahd Causeway change the landed-cost calculation?',
    a: 'King Fahd Causeway changes routing, not the ×1.155 Bahrain stack — its border procedures, commercial timing and fee treatment are not published in the verified snapshot. Treat Causeway-specific clearance and cost claims as LOW/unverified and confirm the current movement with the carrier or broker.',
  },
];

